MXV_TRANSCRIPT = ["Hi, my name is MTG, Your Digital Architect. Thank you for taking the time to watch this training. Grab a cup of tea, sit back, and relax. Today I'm going to explain the Bellver two by two matrix the way I'd explain it to my own family, sitting right next to you. No big words. Just pictures, simple examples, and honest numbers. By the end, you'll say: oh yeah, now I get it. Let's get started.", "First, the big picture. Bellver pays you in two ways. One: a direct commission. When someone buys a card through your link, you get paid once. For example, a Premium card pays you one hundred dollars. Two: a monthly reward. Every month, you get a tiny slice of the money that cardholders in your matrix load onto their cards. The matrix is simply the map that decides whose top-ups count for you. So let's draw that map together.", "Picture yourself at the top of a page. Under you, there are two empty chairs. Just two. That's why it's called two by two. Every person has two chairs directly under them. The row of chairs right under you is called level one. And here's the rule: only cardholders can sit. If someone just registers, but doesn't buy a card, they don't get a chair.", 'Now, how do the chairs fill? Think of people getting on a bus. The front row fills first, from left to right. Then the next row. Then the next. Nobody can jump the queue, and no seat is left empty. The matrix works exactly like that. Watch the numbers. Seat one. Seat two. Then the next row: three, four, five, six. Then seven, all the way to fourteen. Top to bottom, left to right. Just like reading a book.', "So what happens when your two chairs are full, and your friend Sipho also wants a card? Do you lose him? No. The system sits him in the next free chair, further down, under one of your people. You still get your direct commission for Sipho, because he used your link. And he's still in your matrix, so his top-ups count for you too. Nobody you bring is ever wasted.", "Now your people start sharing too. When the person in seat one brings a friend, that friend sits in seat one's free chair. Seat one earns the direct commission, because it's their friend. But look where that friend is sitting. Inside your matrix, on your level two. So their top-ups count for you as well.", "This next part is my favourite. It's called spillover. Have you ever seen a champagne tower at a wedding? You pour into the top glass. When it's full, it overflows into the glasses below. The matrix works the same way. The people above you, your upline, are also sharing the card. When they bring more than two people, their extra people flow down into the next free chairs, and some of them land in your matrix. You did nothing for them, and you get no commission for them. But their top-ups count for your monthly rewards. That's help from above. And it's why Bellver says this is not a binary plan. There's no weak side. You get paid on everyone in your levels, left and right.", "Now, watch what happens as the rows go down. Every row doubles. Level one has two chairs. Level two has four. Level three, eight. Then sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four. It's like a family tree turned upside down: two parents, four grandparents, eight great-grandparents. By level twelve, there's room for eight thousand, one hundred and ninety people. By level fifteen, more than sixty-five thousand.", "So, where's the money? Every time a cardholder in your levels loads money onto their card, you get zero point one percent. That sounds small, so let's make it real. Say your neighbour loads three hundred dollars onto her card, for groceries and petrol. Zero point one percent of three hundred dollars is thirty cents. Thirty cents goes to you. One person, thirty cents. Now think of a toll gate on a busy highway. One car pays a small coin. But thousands of cars, every single day, add up to a lot of money. If your first twelve levels were full, that's eight thousand, one hundred and ninety people. If each one loaded three hundred dollars, that's about two thousand, four hundred and fifty-seven dollars for you. Every month. That's Bellver's own example. It assumes every chair is full, so treat it as an example, not a promise.", "Let me be honest with you, like family. In the beginning, your matrix is small. With fourteen people, it's about four dollars a month. So early on, your direct commissions are the bigger money. Two Premium referrals pay you two hundred dollars. That's almost the price of your own Premium card. The monthly rewards grow slowly at first, and then faster, as your matrix fills.", "Now, a big question. Why a matrix at all? Why would Bellver pay people like you and me? Here's why. First, it's a marketing strategy. Most companies spend a fortune on advertising: billboards, TV, and social media ads. Bellver chose a different way. Instead of paying for ads, it rewards the people who use the card and share it. Word of mouth, from people you already trust. Second, where does the money come from? Every time someone tops up a card, there's a small top-up fee of three point nine percent. A part of that fee is shared with the people in the matrix. So the monthly rewards come from real people, using a real card. And remember, the one hundred dollars you load every month is not a subscription. It's your own money, on your own card, for your own life. Third, it rewards the builders. People who share the card and help others get monthly income, again and again, for as long as they stay qualified and keep their chair. Fourth, it's built to last. When a company earns from everyday card use, it can pay its staff, its partners, and its rewards, month after month. That's sustainability. Fifth, it's teamwork. Thanks to spillover, the people above you help fill your matrix, and you help the people below you. Nobody builds alone. And sixth, it's simple to share. You only need two. Two is easy to explain, easy to copy, and easy to teach. That's why it's a two by two matrix.", "So, how deep do you get paid? Think of a lift in a tall building. Your card decides how high the lift can go. Your stars decide which floors you've unlocked. With a Basic card, the lift doesn't move. You earn direct commissions only. To get your first star, you need your own Premium card, plus two people you refer directly, who each buy a Premium card or higher. That unlocks twelve levels. With a Business card, and two direct partners who each have one star, you get two stars, and fifteen levels. With a Gold card, and two direct partners who each have two stars, you get three stars, and eighteen levels. Four and five stars go to nineteen and twenty levels.", "Now, the one rule that keeps you paid. Every month, load at least one hundred dollars onto your own card. This is not a fee. Think of it like buying your own groceries with your own card. The money stays yours, and you spend it like normal. If you forget for one month, you lose that month's rewards. If you forget three months in a row, you lose your chair for good, and you can't get it back. So set a reminder, and never miss it.", 'Two quick tips. First, get your own card before the people you refer buy theirs. Your chair only starts when your card is bought. Second, payday is the fifteenth of every month. Rewards arrive in USDT, a digital dollar. You can load it onto your card, or withdraw it once you have one hundred dollars or more.', "So, what do you need to do? Five simple steps. One: get a Premium card or higher. Two: load one hundred dollars onto it every month. Three: share the card with two people who buy Premium or higher. Four: help those two do the same. And five: keep loading every month, and let the matrix fill. That's it.", "Quick recap. Two chairs under everyone. Chairs fill like a bus, front to back, left to right. Spillover flows down from above. Every row doubles. You earn zero point one percent per level. Stars decide how deep you're paid. And one hundred dollars a month keeps you paid.", "Now you understand the matrix better than most people. Be proud of that. Now it's your turn. Play with the matrix on my website, download the free guide, and share this video with your potential business partners. We learn best by teaching. If you're still unsure after watching a few times, contact me, or the business partner who shared this video with you.", "Quick reminder: none of this is financial advice. Always do your own research. Rewards depend on real card activity, and are never guaranteed. Thank you for tuning in, and for every like, share, and comment. Your support means everything. And thank yourself for taking massive action today to change your future! I wish you nothing but the best, because YOU are the best. I'm MTG, Your Digital Architect. See you on the next one. Let's Get This Crypto!"]
